# The upstream serves a CORRECT reply, but DRIPS it slowly enough to outlast a
# whole 10-SIGHUP storm, not just one reload. backend-reload-inflight's 40-byte
# value @ 4 B/300 ms (~3 s total) is sized for ONE reload; this scenario fires
# TEN, each costing a prober_signal_wait round trip.
#
# On the timing budget (CodeRabbit raised this): prober_signal_wait is NOT a
# wall-clock deadline -- like every wait in this harness it counts a FIXED
# number of 50 ms attempts (5000 ms budget => 100 attempts), so a loaded runner
# performs the same number of attempts rather than being cut off by elapsed
# time. That is a deliberate anti-flake invariant (see the driver's SEND_SEEN
# loop and prober_wait_listen), so the fix is NOT a wall-clock cap on each HUP
# -- that would reintroduce exactly the host-timing flakiness the counted-step
# discipline removes. Instead the drip is sized with generous headroom over the
# storm's realistic cost. Each HUP is absorbed in well under a second on any
# non-pathological box (a reload's new worker answers within a few probe polls),
# so ten HUPs cost on the order of a few seconds; the 172-byte value @ 2 B/300 ms
# drips for ~26 s, several times that, and still finishes safely inside the
# driver's 40 s bounded, subtree-killing join deadline. If a genuinely
# pathological host ever absorbed the storm slower than the drip, oracle 3 would
# red (held request finished before the last HUP) rather than pass falsely --
# the conservative direction: a false RED that flags "re-tune the drip on this
# host", never a false GREEN.
#
# Gated by backend-smoke: an adversarially-timed reply proves nothing unless
# the same fake is first proven correct when told to behave normally.
proto   memcached
seed    slow    AAAABBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHIIIIJJJJKKKKLLLLMMMMNNNNOOOOPPPPQQQQRRRRSSSSTTTTUUUUVVVVWWWWXXXXYYYYZZZZ0000111122223333444455556666777788889999aaaabbbbccccddddeeeeffffgggg

# on=get:1 so ONLY the in-flight held request drips; the pre-storm baseline
# ordering check and the post-storm coherence leg (get:2, get:3, ...) are
# served at full speed and are not themselves what spans the storm.
fault   on=get:1    action=drip    bytes=2    ms=300
